## 5. Page-by-page detailed spec

### 5.1 Bot Overview — `app/app/bots/[botId]/page.tsx`

Replace the current file entirely (it currently just does `router.replace('/app/builder/' + botId)`).

**Data loading pattern** — copy this exact shape, it matches how other pages in this codebase already handle hydration + not-found:

```tsx
'use client'
const { botId } = useParams<{ botId: string }>()
const hydrated = useHydrated()
const bot = useBot(botId)

if (!hydrated) return <SkeletonLoadingState />   // build a simple one: a few Skeleton blocks matching the page layout
if (!bot) return <NotFoundState />                // EmptyState with title "Bot not found", action back to /app/bots
```

**Layout, top to bottom:**

1. **`BotHeader`** (shared component, also reused on the backtest pages): back arrow to `/app/bots`, bot name (large, bold — not editable here, renaming happens in the Settings tab, unlike the Builder toolbar which allows inline rename), `StatusBadge status={bot.status}`, an "Open in builder" `PillButton` linking to `/app/builder/${bot.id}`, and a `relativeTime(bot.updatedAt)` "Updated Xh ago" caption.
2. **Tab bar** using `Tabs`/`TabsList`/`Tab`/`TabPanel` from `components/ui/tabs.tsx`: Overview | Backtests | Live | Settings. Use the `defaultValue` prop or controlled state synced to a `?tab=` query param so the tab survives a refresh (check how the Builder's existing tab-like UI, if any, handles this — otherwise just use local `useState` seeded from `useSearchParams()`, and `router.replace` on change without adding to history).
3. **Tab panels** — each panel renders one of the four components you're creating in `components/bot/*`.

#### 5.1.1 Overview tab (`components/bot/overview-tab.tsx`)

